          "studentExplanation": "Travelers have always needed support spaces: places to stop, sleep, feed animals, exchange news, wait safely, or continue a route the next day. Earlier forms such as caravanserais, monasteries, inns, taverns, and post houses were mainly tied to survival, trade, pilgrimage, or transport systems. A hotel became more specialized: a commercial place mainly designed to host travelers with comfort, privacy, service, and sometimes status. Nishiyama Onsen Keiunkan in Japan, recognized by Guinness as the world’s oldest hotel, makes the idea concrete: hospitality can become a centuries-long institution, not just a night’s shelter.\n\nThe supplemental notes make the timeline clearer: caravanserais, post houses, inns, and monasteries were not all “hotels”; they solved older route problems such as safety, animals, news, food, and shelter. Modern hotels become more specialized commercial spaces, and Nishiyama Onsen Keiunkan is useful because it shows hospitality as an institution that can survive renovations, changing roads, and changing travelers. Use this to connect travel with support systems: what moves, where it pauses, how services or hubs make movement possible, and why arrival depends on many places that are not the destination.",
          "whyItMatters": "At the entry level, this helps students understand that hotels are part of a long infrastructure of movement. At the section level, it opens Call of Duty-Free by showing that travel depends not only on roads, ships, or vehicles, but also on stopover systems. At the year-theme level, it connects to “Are We There Yet?” because inns, post houses, and hotels are spaces of the in-between: you are no longer at home, but not fully at your final destination either.",
