          "sectionId": "call-of-duty-free",
          "title": "The Golden Age of Travel… or Just a Golden Myth?",
          "guidingSection": "Call of Duty-Free",
          "rawOfficialText": "Some people long for the so-called “Golden Age of Travel”: an era of opulent ocean liners, spacious flying boats, and wealthy travelers dressed to impress. They contrast this glowing picture with cramped modern airplanes and images of bad tourist behavior. Whether or not such a golden era ever existed, discuss: what should be done to improve the travel experience today, and does travel make people act in ways they would not at home?",
          "links": [
            {
              "label": "Atlantic — The Golden Age of Flying Wasn’t All That Golden",
              "url": "https://www.theatlantic.com/newsletters/archive/2025/07/has-air-travel-ever-been-good/683584/"
            },
            {
              "label": "Tourism Management — Tourist misbehavior and informal social control",
              "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0261517720301849"
            },
            {
              "label": "Tourist misbehavior abstract / accessible summary",
              "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/347308508_Tourist_misbehavior_Psychological_closeness_to_fellow_consumers_and_informal_social_control"
            },
            {
              "label": "Overtourism socio-psychology overview",
              "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7305059/"
            }
          ],
          "studentExplanation": "People often imagine a “Golden Age of Travel” when ocean liners were elegant, planes were spacious, and passengers dressed well. But that picture is only partly true. Older travel could be glamorous for the wealthy, but it was also expensive, less accessible, less safe, and not always comfortable. The real question is why the past looks better in memory. Once travel becomes ordinary, crowded, cheap enough for more people, and filmed constantly, its problems become more visible. Tourist misbehavior adds another layer: when people are away from home, anonymous, and less connected to local norms, they may feel fewer social brakes. That means bad travel behavior is not just about rude individuals; it is also about crowding, social distance, status, and how tourism changes behavior.\n\nThe sources stress that nostalgia is doing a lot of work here. Ocean liners, early aviation, and formal passenger culture could look elegant, but they were also expensive, exclusive, slower, and less safe; modern “golden age” campaigns often use manners and dress as symbols for bigger anxieties about crowding, delays, and loss of shared rules. Use this to connect travel with support systems: what moves, where it pauses, how services or hubs make movement possible, and why arrival depends on many places that are not the destination.",
          "whyItMatters": "At the entry level, this helps students separate nostalgia from historical reality. At the section level, it deepens Call of Duty-Free by showing that tourism is not only infrastructure and destinations, but also behavior and expectations. At the year-theme level, it connects to “Are We There Yet?” because it asks whether travel has really declined, or whether people experience it differently once the extraordinary becomes routine.",
          "takeaway": "Travel experiences are shaped by memory, access, class, crowding, behavior, and expectations—not just comfort",
          "subjects": [
            "Psychology",
            "Sociology"
          ],
