          "title": "How Animals Find Their Way",
          "guidingSection": "Home and Wandering",
          "rawOfficialText": "We have Google Maps, or Baidu, but how do other animals know when they’ve arrived? From the monarch butterfly’s multigenerational migration to the globe-trotting of the humpback whale, animals follow astoundingly complex routes around the Earth. Learn more about their migration patterns and processes through the terms and examples below, then consider: are animals truly navigating, or just following their instincts? And is human activity making it harder for them to find their way?\nmagnetoreception | cryptochromes | olfactory navigation | echolocation | zugunruhe\nconstant frequency | frequency modulation | sunlight polarization | spatial memory\nindigo bunting | dung beetle | honeybees | homing pigeons | sea turtles | desert ants",
