          "deepLinkAnalysis": "The animal links make this entry much richer than a list of terms. The indigo bunting link emphasizes star learning: young birds appear to learn the rotating night sky and use it for migration. The sea turtle NSF link is especially important because it explains a “magnetic map”: turtles can detect differences in Earth’s magnetic field and orient toward feeding or nesting areas, not simply wander by instinct.\nThe honeybee and desert-ant examples add a useful contrast: animals can use landmarks, path integration, memory, polarized light, smell, sound, or magnetic cues depending on species and environment. The human-impact angle should not be generic; students should see that artificial light, noise, habitat change, pollution, buildings, undersea cables, and disrupted smells can interfere with the signals animals use to move.",
          "studentExplanation": "Animals navigate through many overlapping systems: magnetic sensing, stars, polarized light, smells, echoes, landmarks, spatial memory, and restless migration behavior. The links are important because each example shows a different kind of “map”: indigo buntings use the night sky, sea turtles use magnetic patterns, bees and ants use landmarks and path integration, and pigeons combine several cues. This makes the debate sharper: migration may be instinctive, but it can also involve sophisticated orientation that human activity can disrupt.\n\nUse animal navigation as a set of different “maps”: birds can use stars or magnetism, turtles read magnetic signatures, bees follow landmarks and polarization, bats use echoes, and migratory restlessness changes behavior before the journey begins. The point is that a route can be stored in bodies, senses, and seasons. Use this to track movement beyond simple maps: what is guiding the traveler, where the threshold or border sits, how tools, animals, memory, or law shape arrival, and why being near home is not always the same as belonging.",
          "whyItMatters": "At the entry level, this shows students that navigation is not only a human invention. At the section level, it expands Home and Wandering from ships and maps to living bodies moving across the planet. At the year-theme level, it connects to home because many animals somehow leave, wander, and return with stunning precision.",
