          "sectionId": "next-year-in-futurism",
          "title": "Can We Still Tell Human Writing from AI Writing?",
          "guidingSection": "Next Year in Futurism",
          "rawOfficialText": "Certainly, here are some examples of text generated by large language models. It’s not just the use of em dashes, but the use of negative parallel structures like this. Wikipedia has compiled a list of signs that a piece of text has been generated by AI. We may soon get to a point where we won’t be able to accurately distinguish human- and AI-generated content—at least, not without setting deliberate traps. Will it matter?",
          "links": [
            {
              "label": "Wikipedia - Signs of AI writing",
              "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Signs_of_AI_writing"
            },
            {
              "label": "HuffPost / article on AI cheating concerns",
              "url": "https://www.huffpost.com/entry/history-professor-ai-cheating-students_n_69178150e4b0781acfd62540"
            },
            {
              "label": "Guardian - students and AI use",
              "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/education/2025/feb/26/uk-universities-warned-to-stress-test-assessments-as-92-of-students-use-ai"
            }
          ],
          "studentExplanation": "As AI writing becomes smoother, students should move beyond simple “spot the em dash” detection. Wikipedia’s list of signs matters because it shows that AI text often has patterns: generic balance, over-explanation, polished emptiness, formulaic contrast, or repeated structures. But the deeper issue is authorship. If a text sounds human enough, readers still need to ask who is responsible for its claims, whether the writer understands them, and whether school assignments are measuring thinking or just finished output.\n\nThe explanation should move beyond “AI sounds generic.” Students should ask who is responsible for a text, whether detectors are reliable, how style can be imitated, and why assessment changes when writing can be produced quickly but not always truthfully or thoughtfully. Use this to audit promises: what future is being predicted, when people expected it, how technical or social limits delayed it, and why “soon” can be a feeling rather than a calendar date.",
          "whyItMatters": "At the entry level, this helps students think about trust and authorship, not only cheating. At the section level, it brings futurism into school and writing. At the year-theme level, it connects to drafts and bright/dark futures because AI can help produce text while also making source, voice, and responsibility harder to see.",
          "takeaway": "Whether source and authorship still matter when output looks convincing",
