          "title": "The Next Frontier Underground",
          "guidingSection": "Next Year in Futurism",
          "rawOfficialText": "“Because it’s there,” the British explorer George Mallory once responded, when asked why he wanted to climb Mt. Everest. (It’s not clear if he ever got there.) During the Cold War, both the United States and the Soviet Union looked the opposite direction and launched rival projects to drill as deep as possible into the Earth’s crust. The United States made it about 600 meters; the Soviet Union, about 12,000—resulting in the Kola Superdeep Borehole. The project only shut down in 1992 after the Soviet Union itself went into a hole. Learn more about this project and others like it, then discuss with your team: what, if anything, should be the next frontier humanity tries to reach—and why? And are races—and heated rivalries—the best way to get somewhere quickly?",
          "links": [
            {
              "label": "BBC Future - the deepest hole ever dug",
              "url": "https://www.bbc.com/future/article/20190503-the-deepest-hole-we-have-ever-dug"
            },
            {
              "label": "Orion Magazine - Kola Borehole",
              "url": "https://orionmagazine.org/article/kola-borehole-russia-scientific-dig/"
            },
            {
              "label": "Wikipedia - Kola Superdeep Borehole",
              "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kola_Superdeep_Borehole"
            }
          ],
          "studentExplanation": "The Kola Superdeep Borehole reminds students that the future is not always above us in space or ahead of us in computers. Sometimes the next frontier is below our feet. During the Cold War, the United States and Soviet Union competed to drill deep into Earth’s crust; the Soviet project reached more than 12 kilometers before heat, technical limits, and political collapse ended the effort. The entry asks whether rivalry helps humanity move faster, or whether it can distort exploration into prestige competition.\n\nThe Kola Superdeep Borehole and Everest-style “because it’s there” logic show that frontiers are not only outward into space. Underground exploration runs into heat, pressure, tools, funding, and national rivalry, so “frontier” means a limit where curiosity meets physical resistance. Use this to audit promises: what future is being predicted, when people expected it, how technical or social limits delayed it, and why “soon” can be a feeling rather than a calendar date.",
          "whyItMatters": "At the entry level, this shows that frontiers are chosen and culturally framed. At the section level, it closes futurism by asking not just what we can chase, but why we chase it. At the year-theme level, it connects to progress and prediction because getting “there” depends on values, not only distance.",
          "takeaway": "Whether competition helps discovery or distorts the reasons for exploring",
