          "studentExplanation": "In early personal computing, waiting could feel like failure: users often had no clear way to know whether a program was working, frozen, or about to crash. Brad A. Myers's research on percent-done progress indicators showed that a visible sign of progress could make waiting feel more acceptable. The most important insight is that people were not only asking, 'Is the computer done?' They were asking, 'Is anything happening at all?' A progress bar answers that emotional question, even when its timing is imperfect.\n\nAdd the experiment context: Myers’s percent-done indicators mattered because users preferred seeing status, even when the estimate was imperfect. The progress bar became a small emotional technology: it reassures people that a hidden system has not stopped. Use this to separate real progress from visible progress: what is actually changing, what the user can see, how the indicator changes behavior, and when reassurance becomes manipulation.",
