          "sectionId": "progress-not-regress",
          "title": "Progress Bar, So Many of Them!",
          "guidingSection": "Progress, Not Regress",
          "rawOfficialText": "Bars are not the only way to pass time. Explore the following alternatives to the standard progress bar, then discuss with your team: what are the advantages and disadvantages of each approach?\n\nindeterminate progress bar | splash screen | console output | skeleton screen | throbber",
          "links": [],
          "studentExplanation": "Not every waiting interface is a progress bar. Some indicators show a percentage, some only signal that work is happening, and some distract or prepare the user while content loads. These patterns all answer the same emotional problem in different ways: users dislike being left in the dark. A good loading pattern does not merely decorate a delay; it tells the user what kind of delay they are experiencing.\n\nThe varieties should be explained by what kind of uncertainty they solve. Percent bars estimate completion, indeterminate bars and throbbers show activity without a number, splash screens brand the wait, console output tells developers what is happening, and skeleton screens preview the final layout to reduce cognitive load. Use this to separate real progress from visible progress: what is actually changing, what the user can see, how the indicator changes behavior, and when reassurance becomes manipulation.",
          "whyItMatters": "At the entry level, this helps students compare different interface solutions to waiting. At the section level, it expands progress from one famous bar into a whole design language of perceived movement. At the year-theme level, it connects to waiting because different indicators change how close to 'there' a user feels, even before anything is actually finished.",
          "takeaway": "One estimates completion, while the other mainly signals ongoing activity",
          "subjects": [
            "Computer Science / Technology"
          ],
