          "deepLinkAnalysis": "The Australian Parliamentary Education Office link is core content because it explains what a caretaker period actually means in a parliamentary system: the government remains in office, handles day-to-day business, and avoids major decisions unless it consults the opposition. This should shape the student explanation more than a generic definition would.\nThe Bangladesh reference in the raw content matters because it shows caretaker government not as a dusty civics term, but as a living constitutional dispute. The 2025 restoration of a nonpartisan caretaker system should be framed as a response to concerns about election fairness, while also raising the risk that temporary arrangements can become politically contested.\nFor students, the key tension is neutrality vs accountability: a caretaker government may reduce mistrust during elections, but it is still a government with real power in a fragile in-between moment.",
          "studentExplanation": "A caretaker government is a government for the in-between. During an election or transfer of power, leaders may remain in office to keep the country running, but they are expected to avoid major new decisions that should belong to the next fully empowered government. The Bangladesh case in the raw text makes the issue concrete: caretaker systems can be used to protect election fairness, but they can also become political battlegrounds about who should control the transition.\n\nCaretaker governments are useful because they show a formal near-end period in politics. Their job is continuity, not transformation: keep services running, avoid major new decisions, and preserve trust until the next legitimate government arrives. Use this to track how endings begin before they officially happen: what is ending, who notices it first, what signs make it feel near, and why people act differently in the near-end zone.",
          "whyItMatters": "At the entry level, this explains how some political systems manage power before a new government fully arrives. At the section level, it opens The End Is Nearish by showing that endings in politics are rarely clean: power can become temporary before it disappears. At the year-theme level, it connects to transition and thresholds because the country is neither fully under the old moment nor fully in the new one.",
