          "sectionId": "the-end-is-nearish",
          "title": "Why People Keep Predicting the End",
          "guidingSection": "The End is Nearish",
          "rawOfficialText": "Midnight was coming. The world was on fire and people were dying of a terrible illness. Surely, the end of the world was near—or so thought Londoners in 1666. It was also near in 1844, 1910, 1988, 2000 and 2012. Doomsday predictions come and go more frequently in the social media age, but people have been predicting (and rescheduling) the end of the world for as long as they could hold up signs on street corners. With your team, explore the following instances of imminent doom and consider: what do they have in common? What leads people to believe them—and what makes a doomsday prediction go viral? The Millerites (1844) | Halley’s Comet Panic (1910)\nThe Jupiter Effect (1974) | Hon-Ming Chen (1988) | The Y2K bug (2000)\nLarge Hadron Collider (2008) | Mayan Apocalypse (2012)",
          "links": [
            {
              "label": "Londoners in 1666 — Wellcome Collection",
              "url": "https://wellcomecollection.org/stories/devilry-and-doom-in-1666"
            },
            {
              "label": "List of predicted apocalyptic dates — Wikipedia",
              "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_dates_predicted_for_apocalyptic_events"
            },
            {
              "label": "10 failed doomsday predictions — Britannica",
              "url": "https://www.britannica.com/list/10-failed-doomsday-predictions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Ten notable apocalypses that didn’t happen — Smithsonian",
              "url": "https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/ten-notable-apocalypses-that-obviously-didnt-happen-9126331/"
            },
            {
              "label": "Hold up signs — YouTube",
              "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FcaHsbfkHA8"
            }
          ],
          "deepLinkAnalysis": "The Wellcome article on 1666 is especially important because it does more than list a failed prediction. It shows why apocalyptic thinking felt plausible: plague, religious conflict, the Great Fire, the number 666, pamphlets, mystical movements, and scientific utopian dreams all mixed into a shared crisis imagination.\nThe Britannica and Smithsonian links are useful for patterns across cases: Millerites, Halley’s Comet panic, Hon-Ming Chen/True Way, Y2K, and the Mayan Apocalypse all show that failed predictions can come from religion, astronomy, calendars, computing, media panic, or charismatic authority.\nThe classroom focus should be pattern recognition. Doomsday predictions spread when fear, uncertainty, authority, simple dates, media amplification, and a meaningful story of chaos combine. The question is less “why were they wrong?” and more “why did they feel convincing?”",
          "studentExplanation": "Doomsday predictions often appear when people already feel that the world is unstable. The 1666 link shows Londoners connecting plague, fire, religious conflict, and the number 666 into a story of final judgment. Later examples did the same with comets, calendars, computers, or physics experiments. These predictions fail again and again, but they spread because they turn confusing fear into a simple story with a date, a cause, and a dramatic ending.\n\nThe supplemental examples show repeated patterns: ominous numbers, comets, calendars, failed prophecies, cult leadership, computer bugs, and scientific misunderstanding can all become end-times narratives. Students should ask why failed predictions often lead to revision, not disappearance. Use this to track how endings begin before they officially happen: what is ending, who notices it first, what signs make it feel near, and why people act differently in the near-end zone.",
          "whyItMatters": "At the entry level, this helps students compare failed apocalypses as social and emotional patterns. At the section level, it expands The End Is Nearish from official risk warnings to viral belief. At the year-theme level, it connects to prediction and endings because people often want the chaos of the present to point toward a meaningful final threshold.",
          "takeaway": "Chaos becomes easier to handle as a story",
