          "sectionId": "the-lovely-and-the-liminal",
          "title": "Lost in the In-Between",
          "guidingSection": "The Lovely and the Liminal",
          "rawOfficialText": "Most films take you from one spot to another, through the lens of the camera, as stuff happens in front of you. But some “found footage” has become famous for losing the viewer in the in-between. Watch one of the most popular of these efforts, The Backrooms (2022), then discuss with your team: did it make you feel uneasy? Have you ever had a moment when you felt adrift in this way—and, if so, where?",
          "links": [
            {
              "label": "The Backrooms - original short film",
              "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H4dGpz6cnHo"
            },
            {
              "label": "Backrooms - A24 film page",
              "url": "https://a24films.com/films/backrooms"
            }
          ],
          "deepLinkAnalysis": "The YouTube short is the real core of the entry. It uses found footage not to document a clear event but to make the viewer feel dropped into a space without stable orientation: yellow rooms, fluorescent light, repetitive corridors, awkward camera movement, and no comforting explanation.\nThe A24 page adds an important update: the Backrooms idea has moved from internet short to feature film, with A24 listing a May 29, 2026 release, Kane Parsons directing, and a synopsis built around a strange doorway in a furniture showroom. That makes the entry a useful example of how liminal aesthetics have become mainstream horror language.",
          "studentExplanation": "The Backrooms makes liminality frightening by removing the normal promise that a space will lead somewhere. Found footage usually feels like evidence, but here the shaky camera makes students feel trapped inside repetitive rooms, harsh lights, and corridors that look familiar but wrong. The A24 link matters too: the idea has become important enough to become a 2026 film, which shows how strongly liminal horror has entered popular culture.\n\nThe Backrooms turns a transition space into a trap. Its horror comes from rooms that look ordinary but have no proper destination, and found footage makes the viewer feel as if the evidence is being discovered rather than narrated. Use this to test the word “liminal” carefully: what threshold is present, where movement pauses, how the space changes memory or feeling, and whether the in-between is useful, beautiful, scary, or too vague.",
          "whyItMatters": "At the entry level, this shows how liminality can produce unease rather than beauty. At the section level, it introduces the darker side of The Lovely and the Liminal: being in-between can feel uncanny, empty, and directionless. At the year-theme level, it connects to “Are We There Yet?” because the viewer keeps moving but never feels closer to arrival.",
          "takeaway": "A passage becomes terrifying when it never resolves.",
