          "sectionId": "where-were-going-well-still-need-them",
          "title": "Why Stroads Fail",
          "guidingSection": "Where We're Going, We'll Still Need Them",
          "rawOfficialText": "If the chicken crossed the stroad, it would probably get run over. Explore what makes stroads different from—and, according to critics, more dangerous than—traditional streets and roads, then discuss with your team: are there any in your community, and are there times when they might be the best choice?",
          "links": [
            {
              "label": "Stroad article",
              "url": "https://theconversation.com/not-quite-a-street-not-quite-a-road-why-stroads-are-disasters-of-urban-planning-and-how-to-fix-them-232485"
            },
            {
              "label": "Strong Towns stroad background",
              "url": "https://www.strongtowns.org/journal/2017/10/30/the-stroad"
            }
          ],
          "studentExplanation": "A stroad is a hybrid that tries to be a fast road and a local street at the same time. That sounds efficient, but critics argue it often fails at both: cars move too fast for a place full of driveways, shops, and pedestrians, while the space feels too dangerous and unpleasant to work as a real street. A stroad is a design problem because it mixes purposes that pull against each other.\n\nA stroad fails because it tries to be a fast road and a productive street at the same time. The result is often dangerous speed, many driveways, weak walking conditions, expensive infrastructure, and places that are neither pleasant destinations nor efficient movement corridors. Use this to treat roads as systems, not background: what the route is built to do, where it channels people or goods, how it changes choices, and why routes remain cultural even after their practical role changes.",
          "whyItMatters": "At the entry level, this helps students understand why some ordinary-looking roads feel stressful or unsafe. At the section level, it adds a real urban-design failure to the discussion of routes and infrastructure. At the year-theme level, it connects to thresholds and navigation because badly designed movement spaces can make everyday journeys harder instead of easier.",
          "takeaway": "It shows how compromise can fail when goals are not truly compatible",
          "subjects": [
            "Design / Architecture / Urbanism"
          ],
          "bigIdeas": [
            "Infrastructure Shapes Behavior",
            "Movement Without Arrival",
            "Managing Uncertainty",
            "Thresholds & Irreversibility"
          ],
